replace(39,['The joint state grows as3+2M and the dense covariance as3+2M entries; filtering keeps a separate raw record of each earlier observation.','The joint state has3+2M entries and a stored full covariance has(3+2M)² entries; the current estimate summarizes past evidence, while a pose graph explicitly retains multiple-pose constraints.','The state and dense covariance both have(3+2M)² entries; each new measurement replaces all earlier information.','The state has3+2M entries and full covariance has only2M entries, because correlated coordinates need no additional storage.'],['A full covariance is a square matrix, and ordinary recursive filtering does not require retaining every raw observation as part of the state.','State dimension is linear in landmark count, while dense covariance storage is quadratic; historical evidence can persist through the joint estimate.','The state is a vector, not a square matrix, and filtering does not simply discard the effects of earlier evidence.','Correlations occupy off-diagonal covariance entries rather than eliminating them.'],stem='In planar EKF-SLAM with M2D landmarks and a three-coordinate robot pose, which combination correctly describes dense storage growth and retained historical information?',why='A joint state of dimension n=3+2M uses n scalar state entries and n² entries if its full covariance is stored. The covariance summarizes accumulated information, including correlations; a pose graph instead explicitly represents constraints among multiple poses. Quadratic storage is not a universal runtime claim.')
